/* reglib2: STATE and LOCAL regulation reference library (CA, IL, NV, FL, AZ + counties/cities Steven works) for the VANESSA dashboard. Plain data, no functions. Written 2026-10-31. verify:true marks entries with figures or statuses not confirmed against a primary source on the write date; fee schedules change each July 1 / Jan 1 and should be re-checked on the recorder site before quoting. */
window.REG_LIB=(window.REG_LIB||[]).concat([
{k:"ca_crmla",n:"California Residential Mortgage Lending Act (CRMLA)",cite:"Cal. Fin. Code 50000-50706; 10 CCR 1950.003 et seq.",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"DFPI (Department of Financial Protection and Innovation)",topic:["licensing","origination","servicing","advertising"],summary:"The CRMLA licenses residential mortgage lenders and servicers that make or service loans secured by 1-4 unit California property and, since 2019, lets licensees broker loans to other CRMLA or institutional lenders. It requires a tangible net worth, a surety bond, an annual audited report, per-diem interest limits and DFPI examinations. In 2026 California added the Fair Lending Examination Act (AB 801, effective Jan 1, 2027) requiring periodic DFPI fair lending exams of every CRMLA licensee. A broker can operate in California under the CRMLA, the CFL or a DRE broker license; most small brokers choose DRE or CFL, so this entry matters mainly for lender relationships and if Patriot Pacific ever becomes a lender.",applies:"Companies that make, service or (if licensed) broker residential mortgage loans in California and are not banks, DRE brokers or CFL licensees; MLOs working for CRMLA licensees need a DFPI MLO license.",key:["Minimum tangible net worth $250,000 (Fin. Code 50201); audited financials filed annually within 90 days of fiscal year end (50307).","Surety bond $50,000 minimum, scaled to $100,000-$200,000 by origination volume (50205; 10 CCR 1950.205.1).","Licensees may broker loans only to institutional lenders or other licensees and must disclose the broker role (50130, 50701).","Per-diem interest may not be charged more than 1 business day before the loan proceeds are disbursed (50204(o); see ca_per_diem).","Escrow accounts: interest on impound accounts at 2 percent (Civ. Code 2954.8) for loans on owner-occupied 1-4 units.","Annual report and assessment to DFPI; mortgage call reports through NMLS (50307, 50314).","Advertising must identify the license and may not be false or misleading; ads must be kept for examination (50204(h), 10 CCR 1950.204 et seq.).","Prohibited: fees not disclosed, delaying closing to increase fees, inducing default (50204).","Servicers must respond to borrower inquiries and follow Civ. Code 2954 escrow rules and HBOR.","AB 801 (Ch. 2026, signed Sept 30, 2026): DFPI fair lending examinations of all CRMLA licensees on a periodic cadence regardless of HMDA thresholds, effective Jan 1, 2027.","Books and records retained 3 years; DFPI examination at least every 48 months (50302)."],broker:"Steven's wholesale lenders are mostly CRMLA licensees, so their California disclosures, per-diem limits and DFPI exam exposure flow into his files. If he brokers to a CRMLA lender he should expect the lender's California addendum and fair lending data requests from 2027. If Patriot Pacific ever funds loans in California it would need CRMLA or CFL licensing, $250,000 net worth and a bond. He should keep DFPI MLO license status in NMLS current if any of his originators are DFPI-licensed rather than DRE-endorsed.",penalties:"DFPI citations and penalties up to $2,500 per violation (50513), license suspension or revocation, restitution orders; willful violations are misdemeanors (50500).",eff:"Enacted 1994 (operative 1996); brokering authority added 2019 (AB 1062, verify); AB 801 fair lending exams effective Jan 1, 2027.",src:[{t:"Cal. Fin. Code Div. 20 (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayexpandedbranch.xhtml?tocCode=FIN&division=20.&title=&part=&chapter=&article="},{t:"DFPI CRMLA page",u:"https://dfpi.ca.gov/regulated-industries/mortgage-lending/"},{t:"CA Fair Lending Examination Act (AB 801) summary",u:"https://www.cfsreview.com/2026/10/california-enacts-its-fair-lending-examination-act/"}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_cfl",n:"California Financing Law (CFL)",cite:"Cal. Fin. Code 22000-22780.1; 10 CCR 1400 et seq.",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"DFPI",topic:["licensing","origination","advertising"],summary:"The CFL licenses finance lenders and brokers of consumer and commercial loans, including residential mortgage brokers who choose it instead of a DRE license. CFL brokers may only broker loans to CFL lenders (not to banks or CRMLA lenders), which is the main reason most mortgage brokers hold a DRE license instead. CFL licensees need a $25,000 surety bond and minimum net worth, must file annual reports, keep advertising on file and follow DFPI's advertising regulations. AB 2116 (signed Sept 30, 2026) extends CFL licensing to small-business commercial financing providers and brokers, relevant if Steven arranges merchant cash advances or factoring for business clients.",applies:"Finance lenders and brokers in California not otherwise licensed; mortgage brokers licensed under the CFL may broker only to CFL licensees; MLOs at CFL companies hold DFPI MLO licenses.",key:["Net worth: $25,000 for brokers and lenders that do not make residential mortgage loans; $250,000 for residential mortgage lenders (22104, 22105.5 (verify)); surety bond $25,000 scaled by volume for residential mortgage activity (22112).","CFL broker may only broker loans to a CFL lender (22004); brokering to a bank or CRMLA licensee requires a DRE license.","Annual report due March 15; NMLS mortgage call reports quarterly for residential (22159).","Advertising: license number and 'Loans made or arranged pursuant to a California Financing Law license' on ads (10 CCR 1550-1557); ads retained 90 days (verify); no misleading rates or terms (22161).","Rate and fee limits for consumer loans under $2,500 (22303) and the 36 percent cap for $2,500-$10,000 loans (AB 539, 2020) do not apply to residential mortgages but do apply to any unsecured consumer loan a CFL licensee makes.","Commercial financing disclosures (SB 1235) for business loans under $500,000 (22800 et seq.; 10 CCR 900 et seq.).","AB 2116 (2026): factoring, MCA and other small-business financing providers and brokers must hold CFL licenses with new disclosure and conduct rules (effective dates per bill, verify).","Branch offices must be licensed; remote work allowed with policies (AB 2305, 2023 (verify)).","Books and records 3 years; DFPI exams; licensees must notify DFPI of changes in control within 10 days.","Debt Collection Licensing Act (Fin. Code 100000) is a separate DFPI license for collection activity."],broker:"Steven, as a DRE-licensed broker with the MLO endorsement, is not a CFL licensee, but he should check which of his lenders are CFL-only (private money and some non-QM lenders) because a DRE broker can broker to any lender while a CFL broker cannot. If he adds commercial or MCA brokering for business-owner clients after AB 2116 he would need CFL licensing. DFPI advertising rules still matter when co-marketing with CFL lenders.",penalties:"Civil penalties up to $2,500 per violation (22713), license revocation, willful violations misdemeanor (22780); unlicensed brokering voids compensation.",eff:"Enacted 1994 (CFLL), renamed CFL 2018; AB 539 rate cap Jan 1, 2020; commercial disclosure regs Dec 9, 2022; AB 2116 signed Sept 30, 2026.",src:[{t:"Cal. Fin. Code Div. 9 (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayexpandedbranch.xhtml?tocCode=FIN&division=9.&title=&part=&chapter=&article="},{t:"DFPI CFL page",u:"https://dfpi.ca.gov/regulated-industries/california-financing-law/"},{t:"MoFo: AB 2116 expands CFL to small-business financing",u:"https://www.mofo.com/resources/insights/261015-california-expands-california-financing-law"}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_dre",n:"California Department of Real Estate (DRE) Broker Licensing and Mortgage Brokering Rules",cite:"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code 10000-10580 (Real Estate Law); 10 CCR 2700-3109 (Commissioner's Regulations)",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"DRE",topic:["licensing","real-estate","origination","advertising"],summary:"The DRE licenses real estate brokers and salespersons; a DRE broker may arrange loans secured by real property for compensation and, with the MLO endorsement, originate residential mortgages. Article 7 of the Real Estate Law (B&P 10240 et seq.) imposes the Mortgage Loan Disclosure Statement, trust fund handling, advertising and fee rules on broker-arranged loans. The DRE also enforces fiduciary duties, agency disclosures and the discipline rules that apply to Steven's real estate practice. Because Steven is both a real estate broker and mortgage broker under one DRE license, the same regulator covers both sides of his business.",applies:"Anyone who, for compensation, sells, buys, leases, or negotiates loans on real property in California; corporate licenses require a designated officer; MLO endorsement required for residential mortgage origination.",key:["Broker license: 2 years full-time salesperson experience or equivalent, 8 college-level courses, exam; 45 hours CE every 4 years including implicit bias, fair housing, ethics, agency, trust fund, risk management (B&P 10153.2, 10170.5).","Mortgage Loan Disclosure Statement (RE 882/883) within 3 business days of application on every broker-arranged loan secured by 1-4 units, showing costs and broker compensation (B&P 10240; 10 CCR 2840).","Article 7 limits on loans under $30,000 (first) or $20,000 (junior): maximum commissions 5/10/15 percent and cost caps (B&P 10242, 10242.5); balloon restrictions for owner-occupied loans under 6 years (10244).","Trust funds: advance fees and borrower deposits must go into a trust account within 3 business days; trust fund records and monthly reconciliation (B&P 10145; 10 CCR 2831-2834); advance fee agreements require DRE pre-approval (10085).","Threshold broker reporting: brokers who negotiate 20+ loans or $2 million+ per year with private lenders file annual and quarterly reports (B&P 10232 et seq.).","Advertising: license number and DRE identification on all ads and first-point-of-contact materials (B&P 10140.6; 10 CCR 2773); 'DRE' plus license number and NMLS ID; no blind ads; team names registered (10159.5-10159.7).","Agency: Civ. Code 2079.13-2079.24 disclosure and confirmation; AB 1345 (2024, verify) and dual agency limits; buyer-broker written agreements required before touring property (AB 2992, effective Jan 1, 2025) with 3-month maximum term.","Discipline grounds: misrepresentation, undisclosed compensation, commingling, fraud, unlicensed activity (B&P 10176, 10177); DRE may fine up to $2,500 per violation in citations (10080.9).","Record retention 3 years (B&P 10148); DRE audit authority.","Broker supervision of salespersons and branch managers (10 CCR 2725); corporate licensee designated officer liability.","Transactions in which the broker lends own funds or buys the note need Article 7 disclosures and may require DFPI licensing."],broker:"Every loan Steven arranges needs the MLDS (RE 882 for borrower, RE 883 for lender in private-money deals) within 3 business days alongside the federal LE, and he must keep trust fund records if he ever holds borrower or lender money. His signs, website, social media, cards and emails need 'DRE #' and NMLS ID. On the real estate side he must use written buyer-broker agreements before showing homes and keep agency disclosures and the 45-hour CE cycle current. Because the DRE can discipline him for conduct in either business, a mortgage complaint can threaten his real estate license and vice versa.",penalties:"License suspension or revocation, citations up to $2,500 per violation, restitution via the Consumer Recovery Account (up to $50,000 per transaction), criminal misdemeanor for unlicensed activity (B&P 10139: up to $20,000 and 6 months for individuals), civil liability for breach of fiduciary duty.",eff:"Real Estate Law 1919; Article 7 1955 with periodic updates; MLO endorsement 2010 (SAFE Act); AB 2992 buyer-broker agreements Jan 1, 2025.",src:[{t:"DRE laws and regulations",u:"https://www.dre.ca.gov/Publications/RealEstateLaw.html"},{t:"B&P Code Div. 4 Part 1 (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayexpandedbranch.xhtml?tocCode=BPC&division=4.&title=&part=1.&chapter=3.&article="}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_bp_10131_10166",n:"CA B&P Code 10131 (broker activities) and 10166.01-10166.17 (MLO license endorsement)",cite:"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code 10131, 10131.1, 10166.01-10166.17; 10 CCR 2758.1 et seq.",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"DRE",topic:["licensing","origination"],summary:"Section 10131 defines who is a real estate broker, including anyone who for compensation solicits borrowers or lenders, negotiates loans, or services loans secured by real property. Sections 10166.01 and following implement the SAFE Act for DRE licensees: a real estate broker or salesperson who originates residential mortgage loans must hold a DRE license plus an MLO license endorsement issued through NMLS, with the SAFE education, testing, background and bonding requirements. A salesperson with an endorsement works under a broker who also holds a company endorsement. This is the legal basis for Steven's California mortgage activity under his DRE license.",applies:"DRE licensees who take residential mortgage applications or negotiate terms for compensation; brokers whose companies originate must hold the company MLO endorsement.",key:["10131(d): negotiating loans or collecting payments on real property loans for compensation requires a real estate license; 10131.1 covers buying and selling notes.","10166.02: no DRE licensee may engage in residential mortgage origination without an MLO license endorsement; employers must verify endorsement before allowing origination.","10166.05: 20 hours of pre-licensing education and the NMLS test; 10166.06: fingerprints, credit report and background through NMLS.","10166.07: annual renewal through NMLS by Dec 31 with 8 hours CE (including 1 hour CA-specific elective, verify); failure to renew makes origination unlawful on Jan 1.","10166.08: Surety bond or net worth via the DRE Consumer Recovery Account satisfies the SAFE financial responsibility requirement (verify mechanism).","10166.09: MLO endorsement may be denied or revoked for felony convictions (7 years, or ever for fraud) or prior revocations.","10166.10-.11: unique identifier (NMLS ID) must be displayed on all residential mortgage loan documents and ads; employers report employment and termination in NMLS.","10166.12: DRE may share information with NMLS and other regulators; 10166.13: DRE may examine licensees for SAFE compliance.","10166.14-.15: annual report of residential mortgage activity (Business Activities Report) to DRE by brokers who originate (RE 866).","Remote origination: DRE allows work from home with supervision; branch locations must be reported.","A DRE broker with an endorsement may broker to any lender (bank, CRMLA, CFL, private), unlike CFL brokers."],broker:"Steven must keep both his individual MLO endorsement and Patriot Pacific's company endorsement active in NMLS and renewed each December, file the DRE Business Activities Report annually, and ensure any salesperson he sponsors has an endorsement before touching a loan file. His NMLS ID and DRE license number go on every mortgage document and ad. He should keep his CE transcript showing the California elective hour and watch DRE notices about CE content changes.",penalties:"Origination without an endorsement is a violation of the Real Estate Law (discipline, citations, misdemeanor under B&P 10139); loans originated by unendorsed licensees are unsaleable and may void compensation.",eff:"Effective Jan 1, 2010 (SB 36, 2009); CE and renewal provisions updated 2011 and 2020.",src:[{t:"B&P 10166.01 et seq. (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=BPC&sectionNum=10166.02."},{t:"DRE MLO license endorsement page",u:"https://www.dre.ca.gov/Licensees/MLO.html"}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_dfpi_advertising",n:"California DFPI and DRE Mortgage Advertising Rules",cite:"Cal. Fin. Code 22161, 50204(h), 50706; 10 CCR 1550-1559, 1950.204-1950.204.5 (verify); B&P 10140, 10140.6, 10235, 10235.5; 10 CCR 2770-2773",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"DFPI / DRE",topic:["advertising"],summary:"California layers its own advertising rules on top of Reg Z and Reg N. DFPI rules for CFL and CRMLA licensees require license identification, forbid implying DFPI approval, bar misleading rate claims and require ad retention; DRE rules require the license number and DRE name on all solicitation materials, prohibit false advertising (B&P 10140) and specifically prohibit ads for loans that misrepresent rates, terms or the broker's role (10235). Section 10235.5 requires the DRE license number on any advertisement for loans. Both regulators take enforcement action on 'teaser' rate ads, missing license numbers and VA/FHA government-endorsement claims.",applies:"CFL and CRMLA licensees, DRE brokers and salespersons, and anyone advertising mortgage loans to California consumers in any medium.",key:["DRE: every advertisement for loans must include the broker's DRE license number (B&P 10235.5) and the DRE name or 'Dept. of Real Estate' on first-point-of-contact materials with license number in at least 8-point type (10140.6; 10 CCR 2773).","DRE: no false or misleading advertising about real estate or loans (10140, 10235); no ads for loans with terms the broker cannot actually deliver.","DRE: team names and fictitious business names must be registered with the DRE before use (10159.5-10159.7).","DFPI (CFL): advertising must identify the licensee and state 'Loans made or arranged pursuant to a California Financing Law license' (10 CCR 1550 et seq.); no statement that DFPI has approved or endorsed; ads may not include misleading rate or payment claims (Fin. Code 22161).","DFPI (CRMLA): similar disclosure of licensure and prohibition on misleading ads (Fin. Code 50204(h)); retention of ads for DFPI inspection.","NMLS ID required on all advertising under 12 CFR 1026.36(g) and state SAFE provisions (10166.10, Fin. Code 22107.5 / 50004 (verify)).","AI-generated and bot communications: B&P 17940-17943 requires disclosure when a bot is used to incentivize a sale; Pub. Util. Code 2874 requires disclosure in AI-voice prerecorded calls (AB 2905, effective Jan 1, 2025).","Unfair competition and false advertising: B&P 17200, 17500 give the AG, DAs and private parties remedies ($2,500 per violation).","Senior-targeted ads: Civ. Code 1770 and Fin. Code 22170 (verify) restrictions on reverse mortgage ads; Welf. & Inst. Code 15610.30 financial elder abuse exposure.","Spanish-language and in-language negotiations: Civ. Code 1632 requires translated contract disclosures in Spanish, Chinese, Tagalog, Vietnamese or Korean when the loan is negotiated primarily in that language (translated LE/CD forms)."],broker:"Steven should put 'Patriot Pacific Financial Corp, DRE #[number], NMLS 1921615' plus his individual NMLS ID on every ad, email signature, landing page, social profile and text template, register any team name with the DRE, and avoid rate or payment claims he cannot substantiate on the day they run. When he or an AI assistant markets in Spanish he must supply the Civ. Code 1632 translations. He should keep every ad for at least 2 years (Reg N) and check VA/FHA creative for government-endorsement language.",penalties:"DRE citations ($2,500 per violation) and discipline; DFPI penalties up to $2,500 per violation and license actions; B&P 17500 misdemeanor and civil penalties; FTC/CFPB Reg N enforcement.",eff:"B&P 10235.5 since 1980s; 10140.6 as amended 2018; AB 2905 Jan 1, 2025; Civ. Code 1632 extended to mortgages 2010.",src:[{t:"B&P 10235.5 (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=BPC&sectionNum=10235.5."},{t:"DRE advertising guidelines",u:"https://www.dre.ca.gov/files/pdf/AdvertisingGuidelines.pdf"}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_hbor",n:"California Homeowner Bill of Rights (HBOR)",cite:"Cal. Civ. Code 2923.4-2924.20 (incl. 2923.5, 2923.55, 2923.6, 2923.7, 2924.9-2924.12, 2924.15, 2924.17-2924.18)",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"California Attorney General / courts (private right of action)",topic:["servicing"],summary:"HBOR, enacted in 2012 and made permanent in 2018-2019, gives California homeowners protections during foreclosure: a ban on dual tracking, a single point of contact, pre-foreclosure outreach, written denial explanations and appeal rights, and a private right of action to stop a sale. It applies to owner-occupied, first-lien loans on 1-4 unit properties and is layered over the federal servicing rules and the non-judicial foreclosure statute (Civ. Code 2924). SB 1079 (2020) added post-auction bidding rights for owner-occupants and nonprofits. Brokers use HBOR to advise distressed clients and to understand why a sale date may be postponed or voided.",applies:"Mortgage servicers, lenders, trustees and beneficiaries foreclosing on owner-occupied residential property of 1-4 units securing a first lien; some provisions exempt small servicers (fewer than 175 foreclosures per year).",key:["2923.5 / 2923.55: before recording a Notice of Default the servicer must contact the borrower (or diligently try) to assess finances and explore alternatives, and wait 30 days; the NOD must include a declaration of compliance.","2923.6: dual tracking ban: no NOD, Notice of Sale or sale while a complete first-lien loan modification application is pending; appeal period of 30 days after a written denial.","2923.7: single point of contact (person or team) once the borrower requests a foreclosure prevention alternative.","2924.9: within 5 business days after recording the NOD the servicer must send a written notice of available foreclosure prevention alternatives.","2924.10: acknowledge a loan modification application within 5 business days, stating missing documents.","2924.11: no sale while a short sale or deed in lieu approved in writing is pending; after a modification is approved the servicer must rescind the NOD/NOS.","2924.12: private right of action: injunctive relief before the sale; after the sale, actual damages and treble damages up to $50,000 for intentional or reckless violations; attorney fees.","2924.17: declarations and affidavits must be accurate and supported by competent evidence (robo-signing ban); civil penalty up to $7,500 per mortgage for pattern violations.","2924.18 small-servicer provisions; 2924.15 scope limited to owner-occupied first liens.","SB 1079 (2020): Civ. Code 2924m: after a trustee sale of a 1-4 unit property, eligible tenant buyers, owner-occupants and nonprofits may submit a bid or notice of intent within 15 days and match the winning bid within 45 days; 2924g limits bundled sales; 2929.45: $2,000 per day blight fines.","Successors in interest (heirs) have HBOR rights under 2920.7.","Mortgage forbearance protections for disaster-affected homeowners were extended permanently by the September 2026 wildfire recovery package (verify bill numbers)."],broker:"When a client in Temecula or San Diego falls behind, Steven should tell them to submit a complete loss-mitigation application immediately because that pauses the foreclosure clock, to request a single point of contact in writing, and to keep every letter. If a client wants to refinance out of default he should read the NOD date and the 2924 timeline (NOD, 3 months, NOS 20 days, sale) to know whether there is time. For investor clients buying at trustee sales, SB 1079 means the sale may not be final for up to 45 days if the property is 1-4 units.",penalties:"Injunctions, actual damages, treble damages up to $50,000, attorney fees (2924.12); civil penalties up to $7,500 per loan (2924.17); AG and DFPI enforcement.",eff:"Effective Jan 1, 2013; permanent provisions Jan 1, 2019 (SB 818); SB 1079 effective Jan 1, 2021 (2924m sunset extended, verify).",src:[{t:"Civ. Code 2923.4 et seq. (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=2923.6."},{t:"CA AG HBOR page",u:"https://oag.ca.gov/hbor"}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_cc_2923_5_2924",n:"California Non-Judicial Foreclosure Process and Notices (Civ. Code 2923.5, 2924-2924l)",cite:"Cal. Civ. Code 2923.5, 2924, 2924b, 2924c, 2924f, 2924g, 2924h, 2924.8",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"Courts / trustees (statutory process)",topic:["servicing","real-estate"],summary:"California deeds of trust are foreclosed non-judicially by the trustee under the power of sale. The process is: pre-NOD borrower contact (2923.5), recording a Notice of Default, a 3-month waiting period, recording and posting a Notice of Sale at least 20 days before sale, the sale itself, and a trustee's deed. The borrower may reinstate until 5 business days before sale and redeem by paying off until the sale. There is no deficiency judgment after a non-judicial sale, and purchase-money loans on owner-occupied 1-4 units are protected from deficiency even in judicial foreclosure (CCP 580b, 580d). Brokers must understand the timeline to help clients refinance, sell or negotiate, and because a recent NOD affects loan eligibility.",applies:"All deeds of trust with a power of sale on California real property; HBOR adds requirements for owner-occupied 1-4 units.",key:["2923.5: 30-day pre-NOD contact or due diligence for owner-occupied 1-4 unit loans; declaration attached to NOD.","2924(a)(1): Notice of Default recorded with the county; copies mailed within 10 business days to the trustor and within 1 month to others who requested notice (2924b).","2924(a)(2)-(3): no Notice of Sale until 3 months after NOD recording; NOS must be recorded at least 14 days before sale and posted/published/mailed at least 20 days before sale (2924f).","2924c: right to reinstate (cure arrears plus allowable costs) until 5 business days before the sale date; trustee fees capped by 2924c(d) and 2924d.","2924g: sale postponements by agreement, operation of law or trustee discretion; if postponed more than 365 days a new NOS is required (verify).","2924h: bidding procedures; eligible bidders under SB 1079 (2924m) may submit bids for 45 days after the sale on 1-4 units.","2924.8: notice to tenants in rental property that the property is in foreclosure; tenants get 90 days under CCP 1161b and the federal Protecting Tenants at Foreclosure Act.","CCP 580b: no deficiency on purchase-money loans for owner-occupied 1-4 units (and seller carrybacks); CCP 580d: no deficiency after any non-judicial sale; CCP 580e: no deficiency after an approved short sale on 1-4 unit property.","Judicial foreclosure (CCP 725a et seq.) allows a deficiency on non-purchase-money loans but gives the borrower a 3-month or 1-year redemption right.","Junior lienholders wiped out at senior sale may sue on the note unless barred by 580b (sold-out junior).","Credit and loan eligibility: GSEs require 7 years after foreclosure (3 with extenuating circumstances), FHA 3 years, VA 2 years; a short sale/deed in lieu 4 years GSE, 3 years FHA."],broker:"Steven should pull the title's NOD and NOS recording dates to compute the earliest possible sale date and the reinstatement deadline (5 business days before sale) when a client needs a rescue refinance or a quick sale. He should explain that a cash-out refinance of a purchase-money loan converts it to a recourse loan (losing 580b protection), which matters for clients weighing a HELOC versus a refinance. For investor clients, he should note tenant notice rules and the 45-day SB 1079 window on 1-4 unit auction purchases.",penalties:"Defective notices void the sale; HBOR remedies; trustee liability; wrongful foreclosure damages.",eff:"Power-of-sale statutes since 1872 with major updates 1990s, 2008 (SB 1137), 2013 (HBOR), 2021 (SB 1079).",src:[{t:"Civ. Code 2924 (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=2924."},{t:"Civ. Code 2924c (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=2924c."}],verify:false},
{k:"ca_fin_4970",n:"California High-Cost (Covered Loan) Law",cite:"Cal. Fin. Code 4970-4979.8",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"DFPI / DRE / AG",topic:["origination"],summary:"California's 2001 predatory lending law (AB 489) regulates 'covered loans': consumer loans of $250,000 or less secured by owner-occupied 1-4 unit property where the APR exceeds the comparable Treasury yield by more than 8 points (first lien) or 10 points (junior), or where points and fees exceed 6 percent of the total loan amount. It bans prepayment penalties beyond 36 months, balloons under 5 years, negative amortization, financed credit insurance, and lending without regard to ability to repay, and requires a consumer caution notice and counseling disclosure. The triggers differ from federal HOEPA, so a loan can be a California covered loan without being a HOEPA loan.",applies:"Originators and brokers of consumer-purpose loans up to $250,000 secured by the borrower's principal residence (1-4 units) in California; business-purpose and HELOC rules differ.",key:["Covered loan definition: original principal of $250,000 or less (4970(b)); APR more than 8 points over Treasury securities of comparable maturity (first lien) or 10 points (subordinate), or total points and fees exceeding 6 percent of the total loan amount (4970(b)(1)-(2)).","Points and fees include all finance charges except interest, broker compensation and third-party fees paid to affiliates (4970(c)).","Consumer caution and home ownership counseling notice at least 3 business days before signing (4973(k)).","Prohibited: prepayment penalties after 36 months or exceeding 6 months' interest (4973(a)); balloon payments within 5 years (4973(b)); negative amortization (4973(c)); default interest (4973(d)); more than 2 advance payments (4973(e)); financed single-premium credit insurance (4979.6).","No lending without regard to ability to repay; presumption of ability if DTI 55 percent or below (4973(f)).","Flipping: no refinance of a covered loan within 1 year without tangible net benefit (4973(h)); no steering to a higher-cost product when the borrower qualifies for a lower-cost one from the same originator (4973(l)).","Broker fees and licensee compensation count; no fees for services not performed.","Arbitration clauses may not limit judicial remedies (4973(i)).","Private right of action: actual damages, punitive damages, attorney fees; rescission as remedy; 4979.5-4979.8 for enforcement by licensing agencies with penalties up to $25,000 (verify).","Assignee liability: subject to defenses if the assignee knew or should have known (4979.8 (verify))."],broker:"Because the $250,000 cap keeps most San Diego and Orange County loans out of this law, Steven is most exposed on small closed-end seconds, Inland Empire manufactured-home loans and small cash-out refinances; he should test the APR spread and the 6 percent points-and-fees threshold on any loan under $250,000. If a loan tests as covered he should restructure because California wholesale lenders generally will not fund covered loans. He should also keep the HOEPA and Reg Z HPML tests separate since each has different triggers and consequences.",penalties:"Actual and punitive damages, rescission, attorney fees (4978); administrative penalties by DFPI/DRE; licensee discipline.",eff:"AB 489 effective July 1, 2002.",src:[{t:"Fin. Code 4970 et seq. (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FIN&sectionNum=4970."},{t:"Fin. Code 4973 (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=FIN&sectionNum=4973."}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_sb1079",n:"California SB 1079 (2020) Homes for Homeowners, Not Corporations",cite:"Cal. Civ. Code 2924m, 2924g(e), 2924h, 2929.45; SB 1079 (Ch. 202, Stats. 2020); SB 1323 (2022) amendments",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"Courts / trustees / local code enforcement",topic:["real-estate","servicing"],summary:"SB 1079 changed how foreclosed 1-4 unit homes are sold at trustee sale so that owner-occupants, tenants, nonprofits and public entities get a chance to buy before corporate investors. After the auction, eligible bidders may submit a bid within 45 days that beats the winning bid (or a notice of intent within 15 days), and bundled sales of multiple properties are prohibited. It also allows local governments to fine corporate owners who leave foreclosed homes blighted up to $2,000 per day. The provisions were set to sunset Jan 1, 2026 but were extended (verify current sunset). For brokers, it changes investor auction timelines and creates opportunities for owner-occupant buyers to use financing after a sale.",applies:"Trustee sales of residential 1-4 unit property in California; eligible bidders include prospective owner-occupants, tenants, nonprofits, community land trusts, limited-equity co-ops and public entities.",key:["2924g(e): trustee may not bundle multiple properties into one sale; each is sold separately.","2924m(c): if the last and highest bidder is not an eligible bidder, the sale is not final for 15 days (notice of intent) and 45 days (actual bid) after the sale.","Eligible tenant buyer: a tenant occupying the property; may match the last and highest bid within 45 days.","Prospective owner-occupant: must certify intent to occupy within 60 days as primary residence for at least 1 year; bid must exceed the last highest bid (2924m(a)(1), (c)(3)).","Nonprofits, CLTs, co-ops and public entities may bid during the 45 days, with priority rules among categories.","Trustee must post sale information and accept bids per 2924m(d); the trustee's deed is recorded only after the window closes.","2929.45: local governments may impose up to $2,000 per day in fines for blighted foreclosed properties owned by entities that bought 1-4 unit homes.","Financing: eligible bidders may use financing but must close within the statutory window; many lenders will not lend on a sale that is not final.","SB 1323 (2022) clarified procedures and extended the sunset (2924m now effective through Jan 1, 2031, verify).","Does not apply to judicial foreclosures or to properties of 5+ units."],broker:"Steven should tell investor clients who buy at San Diego or Riverside County trustee sales that on 1-4 unit homes the sale may be overturned for 45 days and they should not start rehab or lending until the trustee's deed records. For owner-occupant clients interested in auction properties he can pre-approve them and prepare the occupancy affidavit, but he must line up a lender willing to fund a post-auction eligible bid within the deadline (hard money is often the only practical path, followed by a refinance). As a listing broker on REO he should expect trustees to require separate sales per property.",penalties:"Sales conducted in violation may be voidable; blight fines up to $2,000 per day; trustee liability.",eff:"Effective Jan 1, 2021; amended by SB 1323 effective Jan 1, 2023; sunset extended (verify).",src:[{t:"Civ. Code 2924m (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=2924m."},{t:"SB 1079 bill page",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=201920200SB1079"}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_ccpa",n:"California Consumer Privacy Act / California Privacy Rights Act (CCPA/CPRA) and 2025 regulations",cite:"Cal. Civ. Code 1798.100-1798.199.100; 11 CCR 7000-7304 (CPPA regulations incl. ADMT, risk assessments, cybersecurity audits)",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"California Privacy Protection Agency / Attorney General",topic:["privacy","data-security","advertising"],summary:"The CCPA gives California residents rights to know, delete, correct, and opt out of the sale or sharing of personal information, and requires covered businesses to post privacy notices and honor requests. It applies to for-profit businesses that meet revenue or data-volume thresholds, and it exempts personal information already covered by GLBA, but not the business itself, so a mortgage broker's marketing data, website analytics and employee data are covered. Regulations approved September 2025 and effective January 1, 2026 add cybersecurity audits, risk assessments, and automated decision-making technology (ADMT) rules with staggered compliance (ADMT by January 1, 2027). The Delete Act adds data broker registration and a one-stop deletion platform. The penalty for violations is per consumer and per violation, and the CPPA is now actively enforcing.",applies:"For-profit businesses doing business in California with over about $26.6 million annual revenue (indexed), or that buy/sell/share personal information of 100,000+ consumers or households, or derive 50 percent or more of revenue from selling or sharing personal information; service providers and contractors by contract.",key:["Threshold: gross revenue over $25 million as adjusted (about $26,625,000 for 2025-2026, verify), or 100,000+ consumers/households, or 50 percent of revenue from selling/sharing (1798.140(d)).","GLBA exemption is data-level: NPI collected under GLBA is exempt, but website visitor data, marketing lists, lead data and employee data are covered (1798.145(e)).","Consumer rights: know, delete, correct, opt out of sale/sharing, limit use of sensitive personal information, non-discrimination (1798.100-1798.125); respond within 45 days.","Privacy policy and notice at collection; 'Do Not Sell or Share My Personal Information' link and honoring Global Privacy Control signals (11 CCR 7025).","Sensitive personal information (SSN, financial account, precise geolocation, race, etc.) requires a limit-use option.","Contracts with service providers and third parties must include CCPA-required terms (11 CCR 7051).","Risk assessments required for processing that presents significant risk (selling/sharing PI, sensitive PI, ADMT for significant decisions, training ADMT); first assessments due for 2026 activity with attestation to CPPA by April 1, 2028.","ADMT: notice, opt-out and access rights when ADMT is used to make a significant decision (including lending and housing decisions); compliance by Jan 1, 2027.","Cybersecurity audits: annual for businesses whose processing presents significant risk; first certifications due April 1, 2028-2030 depending on revenue.","Data breach: private right of action for breaches caused by failure to maintain reasonable security: $100-$750 per consumer per incident (1798.150); Civ. Code 1798.82 breach notification.","Delete Act (SB 362): data brokers register with CPPA; DROP deletion mechanism launched 2026 (verify); brokers who buy lead lists are not usually data brokers but their vendors are.","Penalties: up to $2,663 per violation and $7,988 per intentional or minors violation (indexed), no cure period since 2023."],broker:"Patriot Pacific is probably below the $26.6 million revenue threshold and under 100,000 consumers, so the CCPA likely does not apply to it directly, but Steven should confirm annually (lead-list purchases can push consumer counts up fast) and should still post a privacy policy, honor deletion requests from leads as good practice, and make sure his lead vendors and CRM are CCPA-compliant data brokers or service providers by contract. If he deploys an AI tool that scores or screens leads or makes any lending-related significant decision, the ADMT rules (from 2027) and risk assessment rules could apply if he crosses a threshold. Breach exposure under 1798.150 applies regardless of thresholds if he holds California consumers' SSNs and account numbers, so the FTC Safeguards program doubles as his CCPA security defense.",penalties:"Civil penalties up to about $2,663 per violation / $7,988 intentional (indexed) via CPPA or AG; private right of action for data breaches $100-$750 per consumer per incident or actual damages; injunctions.",eff:"CCPA effective Jan 1, 2020; CPRA amendments Jan 1, 2023; CPPA regulations March 29, 2023; ADMT/risk assessment/cyber audit regs effective Jan 1, 2026 (ADMT compliance Jan 1, 2027); Delete Act registration Jan 2024, DROP 2026.",src:[{t:"Civ. Code 1798.100 et seq. (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?division=3.&part=4.&lawCode=CIV&title=1.81.5"},{t:"CPPA regulations",u:"https://cppa.ca.gov/regulations/"},{t:"Hunton: staggered deadlines for new CCPA regulations",u:"https://www.hunton.com/privacy-and-cybersecurity-law-blog/newly-approved-ccpa-regulations-have-staggered-deadlines-for-compliance"}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_rosenthal",n:"California Rosenthal Fair Debt Collection Practices Act and Debt Collection Licensing Act",cite:"Cal. Civ. Code 1788-1788.33; Cal. Fin. Code 100000-100025 (DCLA)",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"DFPI / courts",topic:["servicing","telemarketing"],summary:"The Rosenthal Act is California's debt collection law and, unlike the federal FDCPA, it covers original creditors collecting their own consumer debts, including mortgage lenders and servicers. It incorporates most FDCPA prohibitions, bans harassment, false statements and unfair practices, and since 2020 covers mortgage debt explicitly. SB 1286 (2024) extended it to certain commercial debts of $500,000 or less effective July 1, 2025. The Debt Collection Licensing Act requires anyone engaged in debt collection in California, including many servicers and creditors collecting their own debts, to hold a DFPI license. Brokers mostly encounter Rosenthal through client credit issues and servicer behavior, but a broker chasing unpaid fees from a borrower is also covered.",applies:"Any person who regularly collects consumer debts in California on behalf of themselves or others, including original creditors, servicers, debt buyers and collection agencies; attorneys partially exempt; commercial debts (covered commercial credit) since July 1, 2025.",key:["Covers creditors collecting their own debts, not just third parties (1788.2(c)); mortgage debt expressly included since Jan 1, 2020 (SB 187).","Incorporates FDCPA Sections 1692b-1692j by reference (1788.17), so FDCPA standards apply to creditors in California.","Prohibits threats of criminal prosecution, false representations of legal status, communicating with employers except for verification, calling with unreasonable frequency or at unusual hours (1788.10-1788.14).","Time-barred debt: must disclose that the debt is too old to sue on (1788.14(d)).","Requires debt collectors to give the DCLA license number on written communications (Fin. Code 100020 (verify)).","Covered commercial credit: debts of $500,000 or less to businesses with limited exceptions, effective July 1, 2025 (SB 1286).","Validation and dispute rights for consumers; itemization of the debt (SB 531 (2022) for debt buyers).","Remedies: actual damages, statutory damages $100-$1,000 for willful violations, attorney fees (1788.30); class actions permitted since 2020.","DCLA: license required to engage in debt collection in California (with exemptions for banks, DRE licensees acting under their license (verify)); annual report, surety bond, DFPI exams.","Medical debt and student loan servicer rules are separate DFPI programs."],broker:"Steven should not use collection-style tactics to recover unpaid broker fees or cancellation fees from borrowers (no repeated calls, no threats); if he must collect, a demand letter followed by small claims is the safe path. When a client's servicer or a debt buyer harasses them about a mortgage or an old collection, Rosenthal gives California clients leverage that can help clean up credit before a loan. He should ask DFPI counsel whether any collection activity he performs needs a DCLA license (most brokers do not need one).",penalties:"Actual damages, statutory damages up to $1,000 per violation, attorney fees, class actions; DFPI DCLA penalties and license actions.",eff:"Enacted 1977; mortgage debt added Jan 1, 2020; DCLA licensing effective Jan 1, 2022; commercial debt coverage July 1, 2025.",src:[{t:"Civ. Code 1788 et seq. (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&division=3.&title=1.6C.&part=4.&chapter=&article="},{t:"DFPI Debt Collection Licensing Act",u:"https://dfpi.ca.gov/regulated-industries/debt-collectors/"}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_sb296",n:"California SB 296 (2026) Disabled Veterans' Property Tax Exemption Expansion",cite:"Cal. Rev. & Tax. Code 205.5 as amended by SB 296 (Archuleta), signed Sept 28, 2026",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"County Assessors / State Board of Equalization",topic:["tax","military"],summary:"California's disabled veterans' exemption (R&T 205.5) reduces the assessed value of the principal residence of a veteran rated 100 percent disabled (or unemployable) or the unmarried surviving spouse. The basic exemption is roughly $175,000-$180,000 of assessed value (indexed; 2026 figure verify) and the low-income exemption roughly $265,000-$270,000 (verify) for households under the income limit. SB 296, signed September 28, 2026, expands the exemption for lien dates beginning January 1, 2027 to 50 percent of the home's assessed value up to $1 million (verify exact mechanics and any income tier), for a limited period (sources differ on the sunset, 2032 or 2036). For VA borrowers this changes the property tax figure in DTI and escrow calculations and is a strong marketing point for 100 percent disabled veterans buying in San Diego and Riverside counties.",applies:"Veterans with a 100 percent service-connected disability rating (or rated unemployable), blind or lost use of limbs, and their unmarried surviving spouses; principal residence only; filed with the county assessor.",key:["Existing exemption (R&T 205.5): basic exemption about $178,000 (2026, indexed) and low-income exemption about $268,000 for household income under about $83,000 (2026 figures verify); annual filing for low-income tier, one-time for basic.","SB 296: for lien dates on or after Jan 1, 2027, exemption equal to 50 percent of the full value of the residence, not exceeding $1,000,000 (per Bloomberg Tax and CalMatters summaries; verify enrolled text).","Sunset: Bloomberg Tax reports lien dates before Jan 1, 2032; bill-tracking pages say through Jan 1, 2036; verify in chaptered text.","State reimbursement to counties for revenue loss per the bill (General Fund).","Claim filed with the county assessor (BOE-261-G); exemption applies from the date of qualification with partial-year proration; late filings get 85-90 percent.","The exemption cannot be combined with the homeowners' exemption ($7,000) on the same property.","Property tax savings flow into the lender's escrow analysis only after the assessor applies the exemption; many lenders use the unexempted tax at closing and the borrower requests an escrow re-analysis after the exemption posts.","VA funding fee exemption (10 percent or more disability) is separate from this 100 percent-rated property tax exemption.","Prop 19 base-year value transfers for severely disabled persons also available."],broker:"For 100 percent disabled veteran clients, Steven should model the DTI with the existing exemption at closing (if the lender allows) and the larger SB 296 exemption from the 2027 lien date, and help them file BOE-261-G with the San Diego, Riverside or Orange County assessor right after closing. He should coordinate with the servicer for an escrow re-analysis once the reduced tax bill issues. This dashboard's dvtax1/dvtax2 modules should be updated with the SB 296 numbers once the chaptered text is confirmed.",penalties:"None for borrowers; false claims are perjury and subject to assessor penalties.",eff:"SB 296 signed Sept 28, 2026; applies to lien dates beginning Jan 1, 2027 (verify); existing 205.5 exemption amounts index annually.",src:[{t:"Governor's veterans package press release (Sept 28, 2026)",u:"https://www.gov.ca.gov/2026/09/28/governor-newsom-signs-legislation-supporting-veterans-servicemembers/"},{t:"SB 296 bill page (Digital Democracy)",u:"https://calmatters.digitaldemocracy.org/bills/ca_202520260sb296"},{t:"Bloomberg Tax: California expands disabled veterans exemption",u:"https://news.bloombergtax.com/daily-tax-report-international/california-temporarily-expands-disabled-veterans-property-tax-exemption"}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_per_diem",n:"California Per-Diem Interest Limit",cite:"Cal. Civ. Code 2948.5; Cal. Fin. Code 50204(o); B&P 10242.6 (verify)",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"DFPI / DRE / courts",topic:["closing","origination"],summary:"California limits how early a lender may start charging interest on a residential loan. Under Civil Code 2948.5 a lender on a 1-4 unit residential loan may not charge interest for more than 1 business day before the loan proceeds are disbursed from escrow (or the loan funds are available to the borrower on a refinance). The rule prevents lenders from funding on a Friday and charging interest over a weekend before recording on Monday. Violations give the borrower a private claim and are a common escrow audit item; brokers must coordinate funding and recording dates with escrow to avoid it.",applies:"Lenders and brokers on loans secured by 1-4 unit residential property in California; applies to purchases and refinances, including when funds are wired to escrow before recording.",key:["Interest may not accrue for more than 1 business day before disbursement of funds to the borrower, seller or escrow for the borrower's benefit (Civ. Code 2948.5(a)).","Business day excludes Saturdays, Sundays and holidays; a Friday funding with Monday recording violates the rule if interest starts Friday.","Borrower may expressly request earlier funding in writing (for example to protect a rate lock) and the lender may then charge from that date (2948.5(b), verify).","Refinances: funds are deemed disbursed when available to the borrower after rescission; interest may start the day of disbursement.","CRMLA licensees: Fin. Code 50204(o) mirrors the rule as a licensee prohibition.","DRE brokers: Article 7 and 10 CCR 2840 disclosures should show the correct prepaid interest; overcharges are discipline grounds.","Remedy: treble the overcharged interest plus attorney fees (2948.5(c), verify).","Escrow companies typically require a 'per diem authorization' when funds are received before recording.","Does not apply to commercial or 5+ unit property."],broker:"Steven should coordinate funding and recording so lenders wire the business day before recording, never over a weekend, and should check prepaid interest on the Closing Disclosure against the recording date. If a rate lock expires over a weekend and the borrower wants to fund early, he should get the borrower's written per-diem waiver through escrow. He should flag any CD showing interest from a Friday funding with Monday recording to the lender before docs are signed.",penalties:"Treble damages on excess interest plus attorney fees (verify), DFPI/DRE discipline, escrow audit findings.",eff:"Civ. Code 2948.5 enacted 1984, amended 1996 and 2007 (verify).",src:[{t:"Civ. Code 2948.5 (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=2948.5."}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_fair_employment_housing",n:"California Fair Employment and Housing Act (FEHA) Housing Provisions and Unruh Civil Rights Act",cite:"Cal. Gov. Code 12900-12996 (esp. 12955, 12955.1, 12955.6); Cal. Civ. Code 51-52 (Unruh); Cal. Civ. Code 53; 2 CCR 12000 et seq.",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"California Civil Rights Department (CRD) / DFPI (lending exams from 2027)",topic:["fair-lending","real-estate","advertising"],summary:"FEHA prohibits housing discrimination, including in mortgage lending, on more bases than federal law: race, color, religion, sex, gender, gender identity and expression, sexual orientation, marital status, national origin, ancestry, familial status, source of income (including Section 8 vouchers), disability, veteran or military status, genetic information, citizenship, primary language, immigration status and age (where applicable). It explicitly covers financial assistance for housing (12955(e)) and discriminatory appraisals. The Unruh Act bars arbitrary discrimination by any business establishment. CRD regulations adopted in 2020-2022 define discriminatory practices including criminal history screening and source-of-income rules, and the 2026 Fair Lending Examination Act gives DFPI authority to examine lenders for disparate impact under these laws.",applies:"Owners, agents, brokers, lenders, appraisers, insurers and anyone engaged in housing-related transactions in California; the mortgage lending provisions reach brokers directly.",key:["12955(e): unlawful for any person or entity whose business includes real estate-related transactions to discriminate in making or purchasing loans or providing financial assistance because of protected characteristics, including terms, conditions and appraisals.","12955(a)-(d), (k): refusing to sell/rent, discriminatory terms, advertising with a preference, and discrimination through agents.","Source of income: housing choice vouchers must be treated as income for rental qualification (SB 329, 2020); 2 CCR 12100 et seq.","Disability: reasonable accommodations and modifications; assistance animals; 2 CCR 12176-12185.","Criminal history: 2 CCR 12264-12271 limits use of criminal records in housing decisions (individualized assessment).","Harassment and retaliation prohibited (12955(f), (g)); interference with fair housing rights is actionable.","Unruh Act (Civ. Code 51): all business establishments must provide full and equal accommodations; damages minimum $4,000 per violation plus attorney fees (Civ. Code 52).","Disparate impact recognized under FEHA (12955.8, 2 CCR 12060-12065) regardless of federal rule changes.","Complaint to CRD within 1 year (3 years for some claims since 2020); private suit after right-to-sue; remedies include actual and punitive damages, attorney fees, civil penalties.","AB 801 (2026): DFPI fair lending examinations of CRMLA licensees and state-chartered banks/credit unions from Jan 1, 2027 using FEHA standards (verify scope).","Appraisal bias: AB 948 (2021) requires a fair appraisal notice in sales contracts and appraisals and lets consumers complain to BREA; Civ. Code 1102.6g."],broker:"Steven should treat FEHA's longer list (source of income, immigration status, primary language, military status) as the operative standard in California, keep consistent written criteria for every pre-qualification, and avoid any marketing that signals a preferred borrower. Rental-property clients must accept vouchers under state law, which affects DSCR income assumptions. He should include the AB 948 fair appraisal notice in sale contracts and loan packages and route bias complaints through the lender ROV and BREA. Veteran and military status is protected, so he cannot limit programs to or exclude military applicants.",penalties:"CRD administrative remedies and civil actions; actual and punitive damages, attorney fees; Unruh minimum $4,000 per violation; license discipline; DFPI exam findings from 2027.",eff:"FEHA 1959/1980 (housing); source of income Jan 1, 2020; CRD regulations 2020-2022; AB 948 Jan 1, 2022; AB 801 Jan 1, 2027.",src:[{t:"Gov. Code 12955 (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=GOV&sectionNum=12955."},{t:"CRD housing discrimination page",u:"https://calcivilrights.ca.gov/housing/"}],verify:false},
{k:"ca_ab1482",n:"California Tenant Protection Act of 2019 (AB 1482) and 2024-2026 tenant law updates",cite:"Cal. Civ. Code 1946.2, 1947.12, 1947.13; SB 567 (2023); AB 12 (security deposits, 2024); AB 2347 (2024); SB 611 (2025, verify)",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"Courts / local rent boards / AG",topic:["real-estate","origination"],summary:"AB 1482 caps annual rent increases on most residential rental property in California at 5 percent plus local CPI, never more than 10 percent, and requires just cause to evict tenants after 12 months of occupancy, with relocation assistance of one month's rent for no-fault evictions. Single-family homes and condos are exempt only if not owned by a corporation, REIT or LLC with a corporate member and the exemption is stated in the lease; buildings less than 15 years old are exempt on a rolling basis. SB 567 (2023) tightened owner move-in and substantial remodel rules and added enforcement. The law sunsets January 1, 2030. For brokers it affects DSCR rental income projections, investor underwriting, and the realistic rent a buyer of an occupied property can expect.",applies:"Residential rental units in California except those exempt: housing built within the last 15 years, single-family homes and condos owned by individuals or non-corporate trusts with lease notice, owner-occupied duplexes, dorms, deed-restricted affordable housing, and units under stricter local rent control.",key:["Rent cap: 5 percent plus regional CPI (April to April), maximum 10 percent in any 12 months; no more than 2 increases per year (1947.12).","Just cause required after 12 months of continuous tenancy (24 months if a new adult tenant was added) (1946.2(a)).","At-fault just causes: nonpayment, breach after notice, nuisance, criminal activity, refusal to renew, etc.; no-fault: owner/relative move-in, withdrawal from market, government order, substantial remodel (with permits and 30+ days of vacancy required under SB 567).","Relocation assistance: one month's rent (payment or waiver of last month) for no-fault evictions (1946.2(d)).","Lease must include the statutory notice of rights (1946.2(f)) and, for exempt single-family homes, the exemption language or the exemption is lost.","Corporate-owned single-family homes (LLC with any corporate member, REIT, corporation) are not exempt (1947.12(d)(5)).","SB 567: AG, city attorneys and tenants may sue for violations; treble damages for willful violations of no-fault provisions; owner move-in must occur within 90 days and last 12 months.","AB 12 (effective July 1, 2024): security deposits capped at 1 month's rent (2 months for small landlords owning no more than 2 properties with 4 units total).","AB 2801 (2025): move-out inspection and deposit deduction photo requirements; AB 2347: 10 business days to answer unlawful detainers.","Local ordinances in LA City, LA County (unincorporated), Santa Monica, West Hollywood, Pasadena, Santa Ana, Oakland, SF, San Jose, Bell Gardens etc. are stricter; San Diego City Tenant Protection Ordinance (2023) adds just cause from day one and higher relocation.","Sunset Jan 1, 2030 unless extended."],broker:"For DSCR and investment-property files Steven should underwrite rent growth at the AB 1482 cap (CPI plus 5, max 10 percent) rather than market, confirm whether a single-family rental held in an LLC with a corporate member loses the exemption, and verify the lease contains the required notices because missing notices can block a no-fault eviction a buyer may be planning. Buyers purchasing occupied property should know relocation assistance and just cause rules before counting on vacancy. He should keep the local overlays (LA RSO, San Diego TPO, Santa Ana) in mind for rent assumptions on appraisal Form 1007 reviews.",penalties:"Tenant claims for damages and treble damages, AG and local enforcement, injunctions; evictions without proper notice are defeated.",eff:"AB 1482 effective Jan 1, 2020 (rent cap retroactive to March 15, 2019 base); SB 567 April 1, 2024; AB 12 July 1, 2024; sunset Jan 1, 2030.",src:[{t:"Civ. Code 1946.2 (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=1946.2."},{t:"Civ. Code 1947.12 (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=1947.12."}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_tds_nhd",n:"California Transfer Disclosure Statement, Natural Hazard Disclosure and Seller Disclosure Duties",cite:"Cal. Civ. Code 1102-1102.19 (TDS); 1103-1103.15 (NHD); 2079-2079.5 (agent visual inspection); 1102.6c (Mello-Roos/1915 Act notices); Gov. Code 8589.3-8589.4, 51183.5; Pub. Res. Code 2621.9, 2694, 4136; 1102.155 (Military Ordnance); Health & Safety 25359.7",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"DRE / courts",topic:["real-estate","closing"],summary:"California sellers of 1-4 unit residential property and their agents must deliver a statutory Transfer Disclosure Statement describing the property's condition, a Natural Hazard Disclosure Statement covering flood, fire, earthquake and dam inundation zones, and numerous supplemental disclosures (Mello-Roos and special assessments, lead paint, military ordnance, death on the property within 3 years, water-conserving fixtures, defensible-space and home hardening since 2021, and the AB 38 wildfire disclosures). Listing and buyer's agents must conduct a reasonably competent visual inspection and disclose material facts. If a disclosure is delivered after the contract is signed, the buyer may cancel within 3 days (5 by mail). These disclosures also drive lender requirements: NHD flood and fire zones trigger flood insurance and wildfire insurance availability issues, and Mello-Roos taxes change DTI.",applies:"Sellers and their agents on sales of 1-4 unit residential property (including manufactured homes); some exemptions for court-ordered, foreclosure, trust and REO transfers (foreclosure sales exempt from TDS but not NHD after Jan 2022, verify).",key:["TDS (Civ. Code 1102.6) on the statutory form, delivered as soon as practicable before transfer of title; not waivable; buyer's 3-day (delivery) / 5-day (mail) rescission right if delivered after contract (1102.3).","NHD (1103.2): special flood hazard area, area of potential flooding (dam), very high fire hazard severity zone, wildland fire area (state responsibility), earthquake fault zone, seismic hazard zone; usually prepared by a third-party NHD company and also discloses Mello-Roos, 1915 Act bonds, airport influence areas, military ordnance, and other local items.","Agent visual inspection (2079): reasonably competent and diligent visual inspection of accessible areas; findings on the AVID or TDS Section III; 2-year statute of limitations (2079.4).","Wildfire: Civ. Code 1102.19 (AB 38, 2021): homes in high/very high fire severity zones built before 2010 need a defensible-space inspection documentation or buyer-seller agreement to comply within 1 year, plus the home hardening disclosure (Gov. Code 51189); AB 2076 and later updates (verify).","Mello-Roos and 1915 Act assessments: notice of special tax (Gov. Code 53340.2, 53341.5) must be delivered; heavy in Temecula, Menifee, Eastvale, Chula Vista, Otay Ranch.","Death on property within 3 years and HIV status (1710.2).","Lead-based paint (federal, pre-1978), water heater bracing (Health & Safety 19211), smoke/CO detectors (13113.8), water-conserving fixtures (Civ. Code 1101.4).","Megan's Law database notice in every contract (Civ. Code 2079.10a).","Fair appraisal notice (AB 948) in every contract (1102.6g).","HOA documents (Civ. Code 4525) within set times; condo buyers' cancellation rights.","Local point-of-sale ordinances (LA 9A report, Oakland, SF energy and water) disclosed through the local option TDS (1102.6a).","Supplemental Statutory and Contractual Disclosure (CAR SSD) and seller property questionnaire are standard practice."],broker:"On the real estate side Steven must deliver the TDS, NHD and local disclosures on every listing and advise buyers of their cancellation rights; on the loan side he should read the NHD immediately for flood zones (NFIP requirement), very high fire hazard zones (insurance availability and FAIR Plan cost in DTI), and Mello-Roos (special tax in the escrow analysis). For Temecula and Menifee files he should get the Mello-Roos amount from the tax bill or NHD report before quoting a payment. His AVID must reflect an actual walkthrough, and his agents need the 2079 training.",penalties:"Buyer rescission, actual damages (1102.13), agent liability for negligent inspection (2079), DRE discipline, nondisclosure lawsuits; AB 38 non-compliance may delay closing.",eff:"TDS 1986; NHD 1998; 2079 1985; Mello-Roos notices 1990s; AB 38 Jan 1, 2021; AB 948 Jan 1, 2022.",src:[{t:"Civ. Code 1102.6 (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=1102.6."},{t:"Civ. Code 1103.2 NHD (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=CIV&sectionNum=1103.2."},{t:"DRE disclosures in real property transactions",u:"https://www.dre.ca.gov/files/pdf/re6.pdf"}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_escrow_law",n:"California Escrow Law and Broker-Controlled Escrows",cite:"Cal. Fin. Code 17000-17703; 10 CCR 1700 et seq.; B&P 10145; 10 CCR 2950-2951; Fin. Code 17006 (exemptions)",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"DFPI (independent escrow) / DRE (broker escrows) / Dept. of Insurance (title company escrows)",topic:["closing","real-estate"],summary:"In California, escrow may be handled by an independent escrow company licensed by DFPI under the Escrow Law, by a title insurance company under the Insurance Code, by a bank, or by a licensed real estate broker as an incident to a transaction in which the broker is a party or agent (the broker exemption under Fin. Code 17006(a)(4)). Independent escrow licensees must be corporations with minimum liquid assets, fidelity bonds through the Escrow Agents' Fidelity Corporation, and are examined by DFPI. Broker-controlled escrows must follow B&P 10145 trust fund rules, cannot be advertised as 'escrow' companies, and are subject to DRE audit. Southern California, unlike Northern California, customarily uses independent escrow companies with a separate title company, which affects fees and timing on the LE and CD.",applies:"Anyone acting as an escrow agent in California; brokers who conduct escrows in their own transactions under the 17006 exemption; borrowers and lenders rely on escrow instructions and funding conditions.",key:["Independent escrow companies must be DFPI-licensed corporations with tangible net worth $50,000 and liquid assets $25,000 (Fin. Code 17210) and be members of EAFC (17312) with fidelity coverage (verify amounts).","Broker exemption: a DRE broker may conduct escrow only in transactions where the broker is a party or performs acts for which a license is required (17006(a)(4)); may not advertise escrow services, must use 'escrow' with a reference to the broker license (B&P 10145; 10 CCR 2950 (verify)).","Broker escrow trust funds: deposited in a trust account within 3 business days, separate records per transaction, monthly reconciliation, no commingling beyond $200 of broker funds (B&P 10145; 10 CCR 2830-2835).","Brokers who conduct 5+ escrows or $1 million+ in a year must file an annual report with the DRE (B&P 10141.6 (verify) / 10 CCR 2951).","Escrow instructions are bilateral contracts; escrow holder is a limited agent of both parties; lenders' closing instructions bind escrow.","Good funds: Fin. Code 17409 requires escrow to have collected funds before disbursement (wires same day; cashier's checks next business day; personal checks after clearing).","Escrow fee customs: Southern California splits escrow fee 50/50 buyer-seller; seller pays owner's title policy in most SoCal counties; buyer pays lender's policy; custom varies (LA/Orange seller pays county transfer tax; San Diego seller pays county DTT).","Wire fraud: escrow companies must verify wiring instructions; DRE and DFPI advisories; borrowers should call escrow at a known number before wiring.","Per-diem interest coordination (Civ. Code 2948.5) and recording through title's e-recording.","Escrow may not pay referral fees to brokers (RESPA Section 8; Fin. Code 17420).","DFPI escrow licensees must report trust fund shortages immediately."],broker:"Steven should not run his own escrows unless he is prepared for DRE trust-fund audits and the annual report; using independent escrow companies in San Diego and Riverside keeps him clear of 17006 limits. He must never accept a fee or thing of value from an escrow or title company for referrals and should disclose any affiliated escrow arrangement on the AfBA form. On every file he should calendar escrow's good-funds cutoff and the lender's funding conditions so the per-diem rule is respected. Wire-fraud warnings should be part of his borrower communications.",penalties:"DFPI: license suspension/revocation, penalties, criminal prosecution for conversion of trust funds; DRE: trust fund violations are among the most common revocation grounds; civil liability for escrow negligence.",eff:"Escrow Law 1951; EAFC 1989; good-funds law 1990 (verify).",src:[{t:"Fin. Code 17000 et seq. (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayexpandedbranch.xhtml?tocCode=FIN&division=6.&title=&part=&chapter=&article="},{t:"DFPI Escrow Law page",u:"https://dfpi.ca.gov/regulated-industries/escrow-agents/"},{t:"B&P 10145 (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=BPC&sectionNum=10145."}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_prop13_19",n:"California Property Tax Basics: Proposition 13 and Proposition 19",cite:"Cal. Const. art. XIII A (Prop 13, 1978) and art. XIII A sec. 2.1 (Prop 19, 2020); Rev. & Tax. Code 60-69.6, 75 (supplemental), 110.1, 218 (homeowners' exemption)",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"County Assessors / State Board of Equalization",topic:["tax","real-estate","origination"],summary:"Proposition 13 limits the ad valorem property tax to 1 percent of assessed value plus voter-approved debt, caps annual assessed-value increases at 2 percent, and resets the assessed value to purchase price on a change of ownership or new construction. Proposition 19 (effective 2021) lets homeowners who are 55+, severely disabled or wildfire/disaster victims transfer their base-year value to a replacement home anywhere in California up to three times, even to a more expensive home (with the difference added), and it narrowed the parent-child and grandparent-grandchild exclusion to a family home that the child occupies, with a $1 million-over-base limit (indexed). For lenders, the practical consequences are supplemental tax bills after purchase, tax rates of 1.0-1.25 percent plus Mello-Roos in newer developments, and the need to estimate taxes on purchase price rather than the seller's old bill.",applies:"All California real property; reassessment on change of ownership and new construction; exclusions for spouses, certain transfers into trusts, and Prop 19 base-year transfers.",key:["Tax rate: 1 percent of assessed value plus local voter-approved bonds; effective rates typically 1.05-1.25 percent in San Diego, Riverside, Orange and LA counties, higher with Mello-Roos (CFDs) in newer tracts.","Annual increase capped at 2 percent (or CPI if lower) on the factored base-year value (art. XIII A sec. 2(b)).","Reassessment to market value upon change of ownership (R&T 60-67) or completion of new construction (70-74.7); ADUs are assessed as new construction without reassessing the main home.","Supplemental assessment (R&T 75 et seq.): after purchase the buyer receives one or two supplemental bills for the difference between the old and new assessed value prorated for the remainder of the fiscal year; lenders' escrow accounts usually do not cover them.","Homeowners' exemption: $7,000 of assessed value (about $70 per year) for owner-occupied principal residence (R&T 218); disabled veterans' exemption is larger (see ca_sb296).","Prop 19 base-year transfer: 55+, severely disabled, or disaster victims may move their taxable value to a replacement primary residence anywhere in California, up to 3 times (disaster unlimited), within 2 years of sale; if the replacement costs more, the excess is added to the transferred base (art. XIII A sec. 2.1(b); R&T 69.6).","Prop 19 intergenerational: parent-child and grandparent-grandchild exclusion only for the family home (or family farm) that becomes the transferee's principal residence within 1 year, excluding value over the base plus $1 million (indexed, about $1,044,586 for 2025-2026, verify) (R&T 63.2); applies to transfers on or after Feb 16, 2021.","Property tax calendar: fiscal year July 1-June 30; first installment due Nov 1 (delinquent Dec 10), second due Feb 1 (delinquent April 10); 10 percent penalty.","Tax impounds: lenders collect 2-6 months of taxes at closing depending on closing month; the escrow analysis uses the new assessed value estimate.","Prop 8 temporary reductions when market value falls below factored base; assessors restore up to the factored base as values recover.","Transfer tax is separate (see ca_dtt).","Mello-Roos (Gov. Code 53311 et seq.) and 1915 Act bonds appear as fixed charges on the tax bill and are included in housing expense for DTI."],broker:"Steven should estimate property taxes at 1.1-1.25 percent of the purchase price (plus known Mello-Roos from the tax bill or NHD report) for DTI and the LE, not the seller's current bill, and warn buyers about supplemental bills arriving 3-9 months after closing. For 55+ clients downsizing or relocating within California he should explain the Prop 19 base-year transfer, which can make a move affordable and changes the tax figure in the new loan's escrow. For clients inheriting a parent's home he should explain the narrowed exclusion and the reassessment risk before they take cash-out financing. He should keep this dashboard's countytax.js aligned with these rules.",penalties:"None for borrowers beyond penalties for late payment (10 percent plus costs); escrow shortages if taxes are underestimated.",eff:"Prop 13 June 6, 1978; Prop 19 base-year transfers April 1, 2021 and intergenerational limits Feb 16, 2021; $1 million exclusion indexed every 2 years from Feb 2023.",src:[{t:"BOE Proposition 19 page",u:"https://www.boe.ca.gov/prop19/"},{t:"BOE Proposition 13 overview",u:"https://www.boe.ca.gov/proptaxes/proposition13.htm"}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_dtt",n:"California Documentary Transfer Tax (county and city)",cite:"Cal. Rev. & Tax. Code 11901-11935; local ordinances (county rate $0.55 per $500; charter city rates vary)",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"County Recorders / city finance departments",topic:["tax","recording","closing"],summary:"California counties may impose a documentary transfer tax of $0.55 per $500 of consideration ($1.10 per $1,000) on deeds conveying real property, computed on the price less any liens remaining on the property. Charter cities may add their own transfer tax: Los Angeles City charges $4.50 per $1,000 plus the Measure ULA surcharge on sales over about $5.4 million; San Francisco, Oakland, San Jose, Berkeley, Culver City, Santa Monica and others have tiered rates. San Diego City, Riverside County cities and Orange County cities impose no city transfer tax, so the only charge is the county $1.10 per $1,000. Custom in Southern California is that the seller pays the county transfer tax and the city tax is split or paid by the seller depending on the city. Transfer taxes appear in Section E of the Loan Estimate and are a zero-tolerance item.",applies:"Every recorded deed or instrument conveying realty where consideration exceeds $100; exemptions for gifts, transfers to secure debt, certain entity reorganizations, and transfers between spouses or into revocable trusts (R&T 11911, 11921-11930).",key:["County rate: $0.55 per $500 or fraction ($1.10 per $1,000) on the net consideration (value less liens assumed) (R&T 11911).","City rates (examples, verify annually): Los Angeles $4.50 per $1,000 plus Measure ULA 4 percent over about $5.4 million and 5.5 percent over about $10.9 million (thresholds adjust each July 1); Culver City tiered 0.45-4 percent; Santa Monica $3 per $1,000 ($6 over $5 million; Measure GS $56 per $1,000 over $8 million); Pomona $2.20 per $1,000; Redondo Beach $2.20; San Francisco tiered up to 6 percent; Oakland tiered 1-2.5 percent; San Jose Measure E on $2 million+.","No city DTT in San Diego, Chula Vista, Carlsbad, Oceanside, Escondido, Temecula, Murrieta, Riverside, Irvine, Anaheim, Santa Ana, Huntington Beach or any Orange County city (county rate only).","Declaration of the tax (or exemption reason) must appear on the face of the deed; recorder may require the amount be shown on a separate paper (R&T 11932).","Custom: seller pays county DTT in San Diego, Riverside, Orange and LA counties; in LA City the city tax is customarily seller-paid; negotiable by contract.","Exemptions: deeds of trust and reconveyances (no DTT), transfers to a revocable trust, between spouses on dissolution, to a government entity, and certain partnership/entity transfers with no change in proportional ownership (R&T 11921-11930); change in control of an entity holding real property may trigger DTT in cities that tax it (LA, SF).","Refinances record only a deed of trust and reconveyance: no transfer tax.","TRID: transfer taxes are zero-tolerance; brokers must get the city right on the LE.","Recording fee surcharges: SB 2 Building Homes and Jobs Act $75 per document ($225 cap per transaction) on recordings not subject to DTT (i.e., refinances pay it; purchase deeds with DTT are exempt) (Gov. Code 27388.1).","Measure ULA repeal/reform efforts were shelved in 2026; the tax remains (see la_city_ula)."],broker:"Steven should put $1.10 per $1,000 of price as the county transfer tax (seller-paid by custom) on every San Diego, Riverside and Orange County purchase LE, add the city tax only for LA City and other charter cities, and remember refinances pay the $75 SB 2 fee per document (deed of trust plus reconveyance, often $150-$225) but no transfer tax. He should verify the city on the preliminary title report, not the mailing address, because unincorporated LA County addresses with an LA mailing address are not subject to the city tax.",penalties:"Recording refusal; underpayment collected with penalties by the county; TRID tolerance cures for misquoted transfer taxes.",eff:"R&T 11911 since 1967; SB 2 fee since Jan 1, 2018; Measure ULA April 1, 2023 with annual threshold adjustments.",src:[{t:"R&T Code 11911 (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?lawCode=RTC&sectionNum=11911."},{t:"San Diego County Recorder fees and DTT",u:"https://www.sdarcc.gov/content/arcc/home/divisions/recorder-clerk/recording.html"},{t:"LA County Registrar-Recorder document recording",u:"https://rrcc.lacounty.gov/Recorder/Document_Recording.cfm"}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_bot_disclosure",n:"California Bot Disclosure Law (B&P 17940-17943), AI Transparency and AI Robocall Rules",cite:"Cal. Bus. & Prof. Code 17940-17943 (SB 1001, 2018); Pub. Util. Code 2874 (AB 2905, 2024); B&P 22757-22757.4 (SB 942 California AI Transparency Act, as amended by AB 853); B&P 22601-22605 (SB 243 companion chatbots, 2025); Civ. Code 1798.? / CPPA ADMT regs (2027)",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"AG / CPUC / CPPA / courts",topic:["advertising","telemarketing","privacy"],summary:"California requires disclosure when a bot is used to communicate with a person online to incentivize a purchase or influence a vote (B&P 17941), with the duty applying to bots on public-facing platforms with 10 million or more monthly U.S. visitors (17942). AB 2905 (effective Jan 1, 2025) requires prerecorded telemarketing messages that use an AI-generated voice to disclose that fact at the start of the call. SB 243 (effective Jan 1, 2026) imposes disclosure and safety duties on operators of companion chatbots, with a private right of action; SB 1119 (2026) amends some duties from 2027 (verify). The California AI Transparency Act (SB 942) requires large generative AI providers to offer detection tools and latent disclosures (operative Aug 2, 2026 after AB 853 delay, verify), and the CPPA's ADMT regulations require notices and opt-outs when automated decision-making is used for significant decisions, from Jan 1, 2027. For Steven's AI assistant (VANESSA) and any AI calling or chat, the rule is simple: say it is AI, never let it misrepresent itself as human, and keep a human in the loop on lending decisions.",applies:"Any person using a bot to communicate with California residents online to sell or influence; telemarketers using AI voices in calls to California numbers; operators of companion chatbots; businesses using ADMT for significant decisions (if CCPA-covered).",key:["B&P 17941: unlawful to use a bot to communicate with a person in California online with intent to mislead about its artificial identity to incentivize a sale or influence a vote; disclosure that is clear, conspicuous and reasonably designed to inform cures it.","B&P 17942: applies to bots on online platforms with 10,000,000 or more unique monthly U.S. visitors (so a broker's own chat widget is outside the statute but still subject to UDAP standards).","Pub. Util. Code 2874 (AB 2905): a prerecorded message using an artificial voice generated by AI must disclose that at the beginning of the message; CPUC enforcement; works with TCPA AI-voice ruling.","SB 243: companion chatbot operators must disclose the chatbot is AI when a reasonable person would be misled, maintain suicide/self-harm protocols, provide minor-specific protections, and report annually; private right of action $1,000 per violation plus fees (B&P 22601-22605); SB 1119 (2026) revises minor provisions from Jan 1, 2027 (verify).","SB 942 (AI Transparency Act): covered providers (1 million+ monthly users) must provide AI detection tools and latent/manifest disclosures in AI-generated images, video and audio; operative date moved to Aug 2, 2026 by AB 853 (verify); downstream users of such content are not directly covered.","CPPA ADMT regulations (11 CCR 7200 et seq.): pre-use notice, opt-out and access rights when ADMT is used to make a significant decision (financial or lending services, housing) for CCPA-covered businesses; compliance Jan 1, 2027.","AB 1018 (Automated Decisions Safety Act) remained pending in the Senate as of Aug 30, 2026 (verify final status); it would require impact assessments and notices for consequential decisions including lending.","Civil Rights Council regulations on automated-decision systems in employment effective Oct 1, 2025 (2 CCR 11008 et seq.) apply if AI is used in hiring.","Federal overlay: TCPA (AI voice = artificial voice), Reg N (misrepresentation) and ECOA (adverse action reasons must be specific even when a model is used; CFPB Circulars 2022-03 and 2023-03).","Deepfake and voice-clone restrictions: Civ. Code 3344 (right of publicity) and AB 2655/AB 2839 election deepfake laws are not lending-related but show the trend."],broker:"Steven should have VANESSA and any chatbot, texting or AI phone agent identify itself as an AI assistant of Patriot Pacific at the start of a conversation, use an AI-voice disclosure at the top of any prerecorded or AI-voice call to California numbers, and keep humans making credit decisions with specific adverse-action reasons. He should avoid 'companion'-style open-ended chat features that would bring SB 243 into play. If he ever crosses the CCPA thresholds, the ADMT rules will require pre-use notices and opt-outs for AI lead scoring or underwriting assistance. He should re-check AB 1018 status after the 2026 session closes.",penalties:"B&P 17941 enforced through the Unfair Competition Law (civil penalties up to $2,500 per violation, injunctions); CPUC penalties for 2874; SB 243 private actions ($1,000 per violation); CPPA/AG penalties for ADMT violations; TCPA damages for AI calls without consent.",eff:"SB 1001 effective July 1, 2019; AB 2905 Jan 1, 2025; SB 243 Jan 1, 2026; SB 942 operative Aug 2, 2026 (verify); ADMT regs compliance Jan 1, 2027.",src:[{t:"B&P 17940-17943 (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displayText.xhtml?lawCode=BPC&division=7.&title=&part=3.&chapter=6.&article="},{t:"SB 243 signed (Sen. Padilla release)",u:"https://sd18.senate.ca.gov/news/first-nation-ai-chatbot-safeguards-signed-law"},{t:"AB 1018 bill status (leginfo)",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billStatusClient.xhtml?bill_id=202520260AB1018"},{t:"California Lawyers Association AI and privacy guide",u:"https://calawyers.org/privacy-law/ai-and-privacy-a-guide-to-californias-recently-passed-legislation/"}],verify:true},
{k:"ca_notary_ron",n:"California Notarization, Wet Signatures and Remote Online Notarization (RON) Status",cite:"Cal. Gov. Code 8200-8230 (notaries); Civ. Code 1189 (acknowledgment form; out-of-state RON recognized under 1189(b)); SB 696 (2023) Gov. Code 8231.1-8231.20 (online notarization, not yet operative); Civ. Code 1633.1 et seq. (UETA); Gov. Code 27390-27399 (Electronic Recording Delivery Act)",lvl:"state",st:"CA",agency:"Secretary of State (notaries) / County Recorders / AG (ERDA)",topic:["closing","recording"],summary:"California still requires a physical, in-person appearance before a California notary for acknowledgments on deeds of trust and other recordable instruments. SB 696 (2023) created a California remote online notarization framework, but it becomes operative only when the Secretary of State certifies the technology is ready, no later than January 1, 2030; as of October 2026 California RON is not live (verify). California does recognize notarizations performed remotely by notaries of other states under those states' laws (Civ. Code 1189(b)), which is how some lenders close California loans for out-of-state signers. Loan documents that are not recorded (note, 1003, disclosures) can be e-signed under UETA and E-SIGN, so hybrid closings (e-sign everything except the notarized security instrument) are common. County recorders accept electronically recorded documents through ERDA-authorized submitters (title companies).",applies:"All notarial acts within California; lenders, escrow and title companies closing California loans; signers located outside California may use RON in a RON state.",key:["Personal appearance required for California notarial acts (Gov. Code 8202, 8205); satisfactory evidence of identity by ID or credible witness (Civ. Code 1185); notary journal and thumbprint for deeds of trust (Gov. Code 8206(a)(2)(G)).","Statutory acknowledgment wording and the 'not evaluating the truthfulness' disclaimer box (Civ. Code 1189(a)).","Out-of-state notarizations, including RON under another state's law, are valid for California documents if performed per that state's law (Civ. Code 1189(b)); the recorder accepts them with a California-compliant certificate.","SB 696 RON: online notarization permitted once the Secretary of State certifies a system (Gov. Code 8231.1 et seq.); operative date no later than Jan 1, 2030; interim rules on audio-video recording, identity proofing (credential analysis plus KBA) and platform approval (verify current status).","Notary fees capped at $15 per signature (Gov. Code 8211); mobile notary travel fees separate; signing agents may not give legal advice or explain loan terms beyond identifying documents.","Notary signing agents for loan documents are not regulated separately in California but lenders require background screening (NNA or equivalent).","UETA (Civ. Code 1633.1 et seq.): e-signatures valid for notes and non-recorded loan documents; eNotes used by many lenders; exceptions for certain notices (Civ. Code 1633.3).","Electronic recording: ERDA allows e-recording of digitized and digital documents by authorized submitters with AG-approved security; most CA counties e-record through title companies.","Spanish-language and other translations: notaries may not translate; interpreters must be used per lender rules; Civ. Code 1632 contract translations.","Apostille/consular notarization for signers abroad; U.S. military notaries (10 U.S.C. 1044a) for servicemembers overseas are accepted."],broker:"Steven should plan California closings as hybrid: e-sign disclosures and the 1003 in advance, then a mobile notary for the deed of trust and notarized affidavits. For deployed or out-of-state military signers he should ask the lender whether it accepts RON performed in a RON state (NV, FL, AZ and IL all allow it) or a military notary. He should give borrowers accurate expectations (valid government ID, personal appearance) and not promise a fully remote California closing until SB 696 goes live. For Nevada, Arizona, Florida and Illinois loans, full RON closings are available if the lender and title company support them.",penalties:"Defective notarization can void recording or the lien's priority; notary civil penalties up to $10,000 (Gov. Code 8214.1 et seq.) and criminal liability for false certificates; lender repurchase.",eff:"Civ. Code 1189 amended 2015 (disclaimer) and 2019 (out-of-state RON recognition, verify); SB 696 enacted Sept 2023, operative upon SOS certification (no later than Jan 1, 2030).",src:[{t:"SB 696 bill page",u:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/billNavClient.xhtml?bill_id=202320240SB696"},{t:"CA Secretary of State notary public page",u:"https://www.sos.ca.gov/notary"}],verify:true}
]);
